From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 18:10:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] macOS: ask for Input Monitoring, and make the GBA controls
 row real

Field report: a tester's keyboard did nothing at all in a netplay match on
macOS, with no error anywhere.

Cause: the Quartz backend reads the keyboard with CGEventSourceKeyState,
which macOS answers as "no keys pressed" until the app holds Input
Monitoring permission. Nothing in the tree ever requested it, and an
unsigned build a tester drags out of a zip has never been granted it, so
every key silently does nothing. Their other (long-installed) emulator
worked, which made it look like a mapping problem.

- Request Input Monitoring when the backend populates devices, so the
  system prompt appears (and the app lands in Privacy & Security) instead
  of failing silently. Log a clear warning if it is refused.
- Declare NSInputMonitoringUsageDescription so the prompt explains itself.
- Link IOKit into inputcommon for IOHIDRequestAccess/IOHIDCheckAccess.

The launcher's "GBA input" row was informational, which was no help to
someone whose controls were dead. It now reports whether GBA 1 actually
has a mapping and offers a one-click "Use defaults" that applies the
standard keyboard layout and spells it out, since the joiner's GBA being
"GBA 1" rather than GBA 3 is its own source of confusion.
